   "source": [
    "# COGS 108 - Public College Funding and Long-Term Graduate Earnings in the United States\n",
    "\n",
    "Permissions\n",
    "\n",
    "[ ] YES - make available\n",
    "[X] NO - keep private\n",
    "\n",
    "## Link to video\n",
    "https://link.to.your.publicly.viewable.video\n",
    "\n",
    "## Abstract\n",
    "\n",
    "Public college funding is often discussed as a driver of educational quality and economic mobility, but it remains unclear whether higher institutional spending is associated with stronger long-term earnings outcomes for graduates. In this project, we investigate whether public colleges with higher instructional funding per student tend to have higher median graduate earnings approximately ten years after enrollment.\n",
    "\n",
    "To address this question, we combined institution-level financial and enrollment data from the Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System (IPEDS), graduate earnings and geographic identifiers from the U.S. Department of Education College Scorecard, and state-level median household income from the American Community Survey (ACS). These datasets were cleaned and merged during the Data Checkpoint using institutional identifiers and state abbreviations, producing a final analytic dataset of 631 public institutions. We conducted exploratory data analysis to examine distributions, identify outliers, and assess the relationship between funding and earnings. Because both funding and earnings were right-skewed, we used log-transformed variables in subsequent analyses.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our exploratory analyses revealed a positive association between instructional funding per student and graduate earnings. The relationship became clearer after log transformation, with the association appearing more linear than in the original scale. Additional analyses suggested that institutional enrollment was only weakly related to both funding and earnings, while geographic and regional factors contributed modestly to earnings differences across institutions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, our findings suggest that better-funded public colleges tend to be associated with higher long-term graduate earnings, although the relationship is correlational rather than causal and may also reflect regional labor market conditions and other institutional differences. These results suggest that institutional resources may play an important role in long-term outcomes while also highlighting the influence of confounding factors such as geographical economic conditions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true
   },
